course: dictionary
lesson: 10228
title: One word, two jobs
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can be a noun (a thing) or a verb (an action). Toboggan is both: a toboggan is a sledge, and to toboggan is to slide down a snowy hill. Context tells you which job the word is doing.
explain: «Penguins slide on their bellies, called tobogganing, to travel fast on snow.» Penguins have no sledge, so tobogganing cannot mean riding a toboggan. The clues are slide, fast and snow, so here it means sliding fast on snow.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Try «Penguins slide down a snowy hill on their bellies» and see if it still makes sense. If it does, you have found the right meaning.
fact: A toboggan is a long, narrow sledge with no runners and a curled-up front, used for sliding down snowy hills.
fact: To toboggan means to slide down a snowy hill on a toboggan.
fact: Children toboggan on snow days in Canada, the USA, the UK and Ireland.
fact: In everyday use, toboggan can mean any sledge or sled.
fact: Toboggan comes from the Mi'kmaq and Algonquian languages, and the toboggan was first used by the Indigenous peoples of Canada. Safety first: go feet first, and choose a slope away from roads, trees and ponds.
exercise: «Penguins toboggan on their bellies to travel fast on snow.» What does tobogganing mean here? | Waddling slowly across the ice | *Sliding fast on snow, on their bellies | Swimming under the water | Hiding from predators
exercise: What is a toboggan? | A small boat for rivers | A bicycle with three wheels | *A long, narrow sledge for sliding down snowy hills
exercise: Which slope is the safest for tobogganing? | A slope near a busy road | A slope with a frozen pond at the bottom | *A slope away from roads, trees and ponds | A steep slope with a tree in the middle
test: «Penguins toboggan on their bellies because sliding is faster and uses less energy.» Why do penguins toboggan? | *Because sliding is faster and uses less energy than waddling | Because the ice is warm | Because they like the colour of the snow | Because they have no feet
test: What is a toboggan hill? | A hill made of sand for building castles | A hot hill for sunbathing | *A snowy hill used for sliding on a toboggan
exam: In «We toboggan on Saturday», what kind of word is toboggan? | A noun, which is a thing | A question word | *A verb, which is an action | A name for a person
